<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> mittens are warmer because your fingers share warmth in one compartment. Gloves give better dexterity for buckles, zippers, and phones. If your hands get cold, choose mittens (or lobster/three-finger mitts as a middle ground); if you run warm or need dexterity, choose gloves.</p></div>

<h2>Why mittens are warmer</h2>
<p>Your fingers warm each other when they're together. A mitten also has less surface area exposed to cold air than five separate finger sleeves, so it loses heat more slowly. That's why people with chronically cold hands, kids, and anyone skiing very cold resorts often prefer mittens.</p>

<h2>Why gloves are more practical</h2>
<p>Separate fingers make it far easier to adjust boot buckles, binding straps, goggle straps, zippers, and pass pockets without taking your hands out. Many gloves also have touchscreen-friendly fingertips. On mild or spring days, gloves can be plenty warm.</p>

