<h2>The middle ground: lobster and trigger mitts</h2>
<p>Lobster (three-finger) mitts keep your index finger separate and group the other three, and trigger mitts free just the index finger. Both trade a little warmth for a lot of extra dexterity. They're a good compromise if mittens feel clumsy but gloves leave you cold.</p>

<h2>Liners: the secret to warm hands</h2>
<p>A thin liner glove inside a glove or mitten adds warmth and wicks sweat. It also lets you take off the outer layer to work a zipper or phone without exposing bare skin. Some mittens come with removable liners, which also dry faster overnight.</p>

<h2>What to look for either way</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Waterproof outer and insert</strong> so melting snow doesn't soak through.</li>
  <li><strong>Insulation</strong> matched to your climate: heavier for cold resorts, lighter for spring.</li>
  <li><strong>Long gauntlet cuffs</strong> that go over your jacket sleeves, or short cuffs that tuck under them. Gauntlets keep snow out better in powder.</li>
  <li><strong>Wrist leashes</strong> so you can pull them off on the lift without dropping them.</li>
  <li><strong>Fit:</strong> a little room at the fingertips. Tight gloves restrict circulation and feel colder.</li>
</ul>
<p>Browse <%= gear_link "insulated ski mittens", "insulated ski mittens" %>, <%= gear_link "waterproof ski gloves", "waterproof ski gloves" %>, and <%= gear_link "glove liners", "glove liners" %>.</p>

<h2>Tips for cold hands</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Keep your core warm. When your body is cold, it pulls blood away from your hands first. See <%= link_to "what to wear skiing", guide_path("what-to-wear-skiing") %>.</li>
  <li>Dry your gloves or mittens completely every night.</li>
  <li>Use hand warmers on very cold days, tucked on the back of the hand.</li>
  <li>Don't put on damp gloves in the morning; start dry.</li>
</ul>
<p>For a deeper buying guide, read <%= link_to "how to choose ski gloves", guide_path("best-ski-gloves") %>.</p>
